<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kakuyid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1008 CE – 1141 CE (approx.) · capital Isfahan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Daylamite dynasty that ruled Isfahan and Hamadan in the early 11th century, in whose service the philosopher Avicenna wrote some of his major works.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-muhammad">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad</a>, called Ibn Kakuya because he was the maternal uncle (kaku) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid</a> regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyida-shirin">Sayyida</a>, was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> around 1008. As Buyid authority fragmented, he made himself an independent ruler and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> in the 1020s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ala al-Dawla held a court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> (Avicenna) served as physician and adviser from about 1024 to his death in 1037, completing works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishnama-yi-alai">Danishnama-yi Ala'i</a>, written in Persian and named after his patron. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakuyids">Kakuyids</a> fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a>, which occupied Isfahan more than once. They patronised building in Isfahan, including works at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameh-mosque-of-isfahan">Friday Mosque</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ala al-Dawla's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faramurz">Faramurz</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> to the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> in 1051 and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abarkuh">Abarquh</a> instead. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakuyids">Kakuyids</a> ruled there as Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> until about 1141, when power passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/atabegs-of-yazd">Atabegs of Yazd</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kakuyid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Kakuyid dynasty" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Arab_Hafez, edited by HistoryofIran (me). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KakuyidMapHistoryofIran.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1008 CE – 1141 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Isfahan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ala al-Dawla Muhammad, Faramurz, Garshasp I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kakuyids, Kakwayhids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1074&amp;focus=kakuyid-dynasty" aria-label="Kakuyid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6006 991 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kakuyid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6326 1247L6367 1352M6326 1247L6481 1127M6326 1247L6400 1274"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6367 1352h0M6481 1127h0M6400 1274h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Isfahan. <a href="/atlas?year=1074&amp;focus=kakuyid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
